   "url": "/projects/fieldops-ai",
   "text": "FieldOps (Systems and backend engineering; Prototype; May 2026). Workforce platform for a field team of about 20: GPS attendance from an Android foreground service, driver and retailer onboarding, call logs, HR records and a points engine whose weights live in the database.\nStack: TypeScript, Express, Sequelize, MariaDB, Socket.io, Next.js, Flutter.\nBuilt for Movigo; company code.\nTesting and evaluation: GPS points are uploaded in one batch a minute instead of one request per point.\nArchitecture (The FieldOps platform): Staff app: GPS attendance, driver and retailer onboarding, call logs, payments, leaderboard and notices. An Android foreground service batches GPS points locally and retries failed uploads, so attendance holds up on patchy connections and aggressive battery savers. API: Attendance, onboarding, reports and notices for the field team. GPS points arrive in one batch a minute instead of one request per point. Points engine: Scores actions such as an on-time check-in, a driver onboarded or a payment collected. Weights live in the database and reset monthly on a schedule, so managers change the rules without a deploy. Manager dashboard: Attendance and a live map, expenses, salary and targets, and app configuration. MariaDB: A 27-entity schema. Socket.io: Pushes updates to the dashboard as they happen.\nWalkthroughs: “A field officer checks in”: The officer checks in; GPS points are batched on the phone. The batch arrives, one upload a minute. Attendance is recorded. The check-in counts for points. An on-time check-in scores with the current weights. The manager sees it on the attendance board and live map.\nFieldOps: attendance and performance for a field team\nA three-part workforce platform for a field team of about twenty: a Flutter app for staff, a Next.js dashboard for managers and a TypeScript API behind both.\nContext: Built for Movigo’s field staff, who onboard drivers and retailers, log calls and collect payments across the city.\nRole: Designed and built alone.\nStatus: Pilot build for Movigo’s field team: GPS attendance, background location and onboarding work end to end.\nPlatform map: Staff app (Flutter): GPS attendance, Driver and retailer onboarding, Call logs and payments, Leaderboard and notices; API (TypeScript): 27-entity schema (Sequelize on MariaDB), Socket.io updates, Points engine; Manager dashboard (Next.js): Attendance and live map, Expenses, salary and targets, App configuration\nDecision: Location that survives Android. An Android foreground service batches GPS points locally and retries uploads that fail, so attendance holds up on patchy connections and aggressive battery savers.\nDecision: A points engine managers can tune. Twenty scorable actions (on-time check-in, driver onboarded, payment collected and so on) with weights stored in the database and monthly resets on a schedule, so the rules change without a deploy."
