   "id": "hyperlocal-marketplace",
   "title": "Hyperlocal Marketplace",
   "url": "/projects/hyperlocal-marketplace",
   "text": "Hyperlocal Marketplace (Systems and backend engineering; Working; Sep 2026). Customers order from local Indore shops and riders deliver in about 30 minutes. The order state machine survives races, payment webhooks and server restarts.\nStack: Node.js, Express, PostgreSQL, Drizzle, Socket.io, React, Next.js, TypeScript.\nBuilt for Movigo; company code.\n52 backend tests, all passing.\nTesting and evaluation: 52 backend tests, all passing when re-run on 19 October 2026, covering the state machine, checkout and payments, dispatch retry and escalation, restart recovery and webhooks.\nTesting and evaluation: Stock is reserved atomically and every state change is a conditional update.\nArchitecture (An order, from checkout to doorstep): Customer web app: Customers order from local shops for delivery in about thirty minutes. Checkout: Reserves stock and creates the order. Abandoned checkouts release their stock after fifteen minutes. Razorpay: UPI and card payments. Orders become real only after the payment is captured, so shops never see unpaid orders. Store confirms: The shop accepts the order from its dashboard. Rider dispatch: Assigns a rider, retries after 180 seconds and escalates after three tries. Delivered: The rider picks up and delivers; the customer follows each state. PostgreSQL: Orders and stock. Every state change is an UPDATE ... WHERE status = expected, and each transition checks who may make it, so a payment webhook racing a store click cannot apply twice. Recovery: Reconciles every in-flight order at startup: flags stale ones, re-dispatches unassigned ones and re-attaches active rides. A watchdog repeats it every minute.\nWalkthroughs: “Groceries by UPI”: The customer checks out. Stock is reserved atomically. The UPI payment is captured; only now does the order become real. The shop confirms within two minutes. A rider is assigned. Picked up, on the way, delivered: each a conditional update. | The server restarts mid-delivery: On boot, every in-flight order is reconciled. Unassigned orders are re-dispatched and active rides re-attached. The delivery carries on.\nHyperlocal Marketplace: an order that survives a server restart\nCustomers order from local shops and riders deliver in about thirty minutes. Most of the work is in what happens when things go wrong: a shop ignores an order, a rider goes offline, a webhook races a button press, the server restarts mid-delivery.\nContext: A customer web app, a store dashboard and an operations API for a delivery marketplace in Indore, with payments and rider dispatch integrated.\nRole: Designed and built alone.\nStatus: Working build; all 52 backend tests pass.\nOrder lifecycle: Placed (stock reserved atomically) -> Store confirmed (auto-cancel after 2 minutes) -> Rider dispatched (retry after 180 s, escalate after 3) -> Picked up -> On the way -> Delivered\nDecision: Transitions are compare-and-set. Every state change is an UPDATE ... WHERE status = expected, and each transition checks who is allowed to make it. A payment webhook racing a store click cannot apply twice.\nDecision: Paid before visible. UPI and card orders only become real orders after the payment is captured, so shops never see unpaid orders; abandoned checkouts release their stock after fifteen minutes.\nDecision: Recovery on boot. At startup every in-flight order is reconciled: stale ones are flagged for review, unassigned ones are re-dispatched and active rides re-attached. A watchdog repeats the check every minute, and the health endpoint reports it."
